1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,602
My PDF tables are completely messed up.

2
00:00:03,902 --> 00:00:05,353
How would you even know?

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:13,207
A fee table, as printed on page four: twenty-five euros per wire, waived above ten thousand.

4
00:00:13,507 --> 00:00:17,903
The parser flattens the table, and the condition lands in the wrong place.

5
00:00:18,203 --> 00:00:22,406
The model reads clean-looking text, and answers with full confidence.

6
00:00:22,706 --> 00:00:26,120
Nothing in the pipeline says the extraction was ever checked.

7
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:33,398
QualityRag's rule is simple: an extraction nobody has checked is unknown, not support.

8
00:00:33,698 --> 00:00:37,731
The parsed cell is marked unknown, so the check is inconclusive.

9
00:00:38,031 --> 00:00:42,106
After a reviewer compares it with the page, the same cell can count.

10
00:00:43,467 --> 00:00:52,129
In the research track, Semantic Codex indexes documents with an anchor for every value: page, row, paragraph, or time.

11
00:00:52,429 --> 00:01:00,664
It says plainly when extraction is partial, and it does not do OCR yet. Checking table structure itself is still research.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,665
That's the honest boundary.

13
00:01:03,965 --> 00:01:11,667
QualityRag won't let an unchecked number pass as evidence. But it doesn't yet repair or validate the table itself.

14
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:14,719
You've finished the first five Academy episodes.
